## Before You Install

You may upset your anti-virus tools running the install commands even though
we have tried to use AV-friendly methods.  If you have problems you may either
disable your AV software or run the steps below manually.  bitdefender, at least,
seems to have no problems when everything is run manually but sometimes complains
on the quick install methods.  Or you can disable first and then install.  Your
choice.

These tools require python3 and the python venv module for setting up and using
virtual environments.  Generally, venv comes with python but on some systems
may need to be installed separately.  The system has been tested with python3.9 
and python 3.11.  It should be pretty flexible about exactly which version you
have installed but might be unhappy with very old or very new versions.

On a linux-based system, you need to be a member of the dialout group.  See the
Linux/MacOS installation guide below for more details.

If using a Raspberry Pi Pico system, you will need to have Micropython installed
on the Pico prior to installing our software.  Details on how to do this are all
over the interweb with some notes in the Pico/README file.

If using Pololu Maestro animation controllers, you will need to have Pololu's
MaestroControlCenter available.  It doesn't have to be on the same system as
long as it is available to you somewhere.  More details are in the Pololu/README
file.

## Installation

Read the following sections for details on the installation process but
also read the Quick Install section to see if you can use the easy
method.

### Quick Install Option

Once you have downloaded and unzipped the appropriate release file, scripts are
available to aid the installation process.   These scripts do all the
below steps for either Pico or Pololu, except install MicroPython on the Pico and
install MaestroControlCenter for Pololu Maestros.  These
assume a default tabledefs configuration file.  Since most users will work on a 
single platform with a single type of animation controller, running the appropriate
install script is the easiest way to get going:

| OS | Controller | Script |
|------|------|------|
| linux/MacOS | Pico | 1031_Hauntimator/quickinstall_Pico |
| linux/MacOS | Pololu | 1031_Hauntimator/quickinstall_Pololu |
| Windows 11 | Pico | 1031_Hauntimator\\winquickinstall_Pico.bat |
| Windows 11 | Pololu | 1031_Hauntimator\\winquickinstall_Pololu.bat |


To support multiple hardware types from
the same system, simply unzip the zip file again and run another quickinstall
script to support that additional hardware type.  Each installation changes the name
of 1031_Hauntimator to Pico_Hauntimator or Pololu_Hauntimator and creates desktop
shortcuts named Pico or Pololu for running the Hauntimator application for that
hardware.

[ TMI Warning - If you are wondering why we change the name of the 1031_Hauntimator directory,
it is because the desktop shortcuts contain a path to the executables.  These must be 
known when the shortcuts are created.  Then, if the directory name is not changed, any
additional hardware installations (e.g. supporting both Pico and Pololu) will simply
point at the same executables and everything gets confused.]

These quick install scripts only install the python tools.  When using a Pico-based
animation controller, it is also necessary to install a MicroPython image and then
all the MicroPython code into the Pico flash memory.  See the Pico/README for more
on installing MicroPython on the Pico.  Once that is done, the embedded code can be
installed.  There is one command to do this:

| OS | Script |
|------|------|
| linux/MacOS | Pico/Pico_install |
| Windows 11  | Pico\\winPicoinstall.bat |

### Linux and MacOS

If you are reading this, you have already downloaded and unzipped the
release files.  To enable the applications, run the install script in
the same directory as this README.  Run it in a terminal window as it
asks if you want desktop icons created.  Select anything other than 'y'
if you prefer command line operation as I do.

Hauntimator and other applications generally work best
when they are able to communicate with and control the animatronics
controller.  This requires that the user run tools that
access the controller hardware over the USB serial port.  This requires system
permissions such that the user is in the dialout group.  Check with:

groups

If the list that is output includes dialout you are golden.  If not,
you have to add yourself to the dialout group with this command:

sudo usermod -aG dialout `whoami`

Then you have to at least log out and back in or restart the system.

Once you are in the dialout group, proceed with the following steps to install
the embedded software into the flash memory of the Raspberry Pi
Pico:

- Plug a USB cable into the Pico board and this PC
- Install an appropriate MicroPython image on the board (see Pico/README)
- cd to the Pico/lib directory
- copy tabledefs_template to tabledefs and edit it as needed (see Pico/lib/README)
- cd to the Pico directory
- run "do_install" to link to the Pico libraries
- run "Pico_install" to install all the embedded software on the Pico (see Pico/README)

Note that it is not necessary for Hauntimator and joysticking to
communicate with the Pico.  It is perfectly possible to create
animation control files, write them to an SD card on the PC, and 
then transfer the SD card to the controller.  However, a direct
connection provides for more rapid iterations when developing and
testing an animation.

If you are using Pololu Maestros, the steps are similar but different.  In that case,
do this:

- cd to the Pololu/lib directory
- copy tabledefs_template to tabledefs and edit it as needed (see Pololu/lib/README)
- cd to the Pololu directory
- run "do_install" to link to the Pololu libraries

To use the Maestro controllers, they must be configured for your system.  This
is done with the MaestroControlCenter application available from Pololu.  You
will need this application so follow the directions at:
https://www.pololu.com/product/1350/resources

